TODO
====

What is not done, not known or only guessed. The source marks its guesses
HEURISTIC and its stand-ins HACK: each one has its lines here.


GENERAL
-------

Add support for "required" field in the JSON file.

One language: every file is C++20, built with the same warnings, and the
frames of Valkyrie (machine, device, CPU, buffer, memory map) are gone: the
board is a class (src/odin/hikaru.cpp), the window another
(src/odin/display.cpp). What is left of them:
- the save states keep their layout (hikaru_state.cpp): the SH-4 as the
  interpreter of Valkyrie laid it out, and four words of the board that
  are always zero. A new version of the format can drop them; the states
  made before would then be refused.
- the messages of the start ("loading game list from", "Loading section",
  "Running", "Finalizing") and of F1 and F4 have no end of line;
- Odin ends with the status 0 when a game cannot be loaded;
- while paused (Space) the main loop spins.

Speed: the frames are held at 60 per second (-u lifts the limit). On a
Ryzen 5 5600X with a GeForce RTX 3080 the whole demo loops, from power-on
and without the limit, never fall under 60 in any second: the slowest second
runs at 64 frames per second in PHARRIER, 70 in AIRTRIX and BRAVEFF, 74 in
VON4, 96 in SGNASCAR and 112 in PODRACE (two runs each). A run resumed from
a saved state reads higher for its first seconds: the card lowers its clock
some 3.5 s after the program starts. In a heavy scene of PHARRIER a
frame takes 13 ms: the master SH-4 4.2 (1 to 2 million instructions run a
frame, about 3 ns each), the two sound boards 4.3, the display lists 0.9,
the MIE 0.4, the slave 0.2 and the renderer 3. A slower graphics card is
another matter: on an Intel HD 520 the translucent fragments of a picture
like the worst of BRAVEFF take 59 ms, against 0.8 ms on the RTX 3080. The main
loop of the sound driver turns 312 times a frame and an interrupt comes every
22 samples: the turns in between are counted without being run
(src/odin/arm7.cpp). The drivers of VON4 and PHARRIER count their turns in
the word at 0x40 of the wave memory: a turn that only adds a constant to one
word, and looks at it nowhere else, is counted too, and the word is given
the turns it is owed before anything reads it (Arm7::turn_counts; their
sound boards cost 2.4 times less). The turn of the driver of BRAVEFF serves
one of eight slots (the byte at 0x9312 goes from 0 to 7): the eight turns
that bring it back are recorded as one. A turn that was counted is kept
through the interrupts: when the program is back at its start with the
registers it can see as they were, what it read and what it leaves in the
memory still there, it is counted again without being run (Arm7::
turn_rejoin; the sound boards cost 1.2 to 1.8 times less again).
The program of the MIE waits in a loop that reads a byte of its memory,
three instructions out of four: once a turn has left every register as it
was, with nothing written and no port used, the turns left before the end of
the line are counted without being run (Mie::wait_check; the MIE costs 1.5
to 1.8 times less).
ODIN_TURNS=0 runs every turn: the memory, the states and the pictures must
be the same. What is left of the sound boards is mostly what each of the
1 470 samples of a frame costs by itself, turns counted or not.

The frames are drawn on a thread of their own (src/odin/worker.hpp,
src/odin/display.cpp) while the machine runs the next one: without the limit
the slowest second of a heavy scene goes from 72 to 90 frames per second in
PHARRIER, 78 to 98 in VON4, 91 to 109 in BRAVEFF, 98 to 139 in AIRTRIX. The
drawing reads the texture RAM and the display lists of the last two frames;
all the rest is taken from the machine when its frame ends
(Renderer::take_frame), and the machine waits for the drawing before it
writes the texture RAM. ODIN_LATE_DRAW=1 is the test of it: each drawing is
held until the machine has run the whole next frame, and the pictures must
come out the same (the six demo loops, 108600 pictures; reading the layers
at the drawing instead, or building the lists in two buffers, changes
them). Not run under a race detector: -fsanitize=thread falls over in the
NVIDIA driver (a thread of the driver it does not know) and in llvmpipe
(in LLVM). No frame is skipped: each list feeds the 3D buffers of the
next, so a graphics card slower than the machine slows the machine down.
Stutters: ODIN_SPEED=1 tells the longest frame of each second. With the
limit at 60 a game stuttered each time it drew with a mesh program for the
first time on a computer: the NVIDIA driver 595 takes some 20 ms to compile
one, and 10 more when it first draws, on the thread that draws (a scene
change of PHARRIER: 21 programs, a frame of 449 ms). The programs a game
draws with are now listed (shaders/<game>.txt beside vk-games.json for the
demo loops, then ~/.cache/odin/shaders/<game>.txt for what a player meets)
and made ready when the game starts: compiled, and drawn with once. The
longest frame of that scene is then 21 ms, the driver cache empty or not; a
program met for the first time still costs its 30 ms once. The lists start
with a line that names their format (LIST_HEADER in renderer_programs.cpp):
its number goes up when MeshVariant::key changes, and shaders/ is then made
again (the six loops with an empty XDG_CACHE_HOME). What is left, in a
heavy scene of each game at 60 frames per second: no frame late in
PHARRIER, PODRACE and VON4, one or two in 30 s in AIRTRIX, BRAVEFF and
SGNASCAR, 4 to 8 ms late. In BRAVEFF (frame 3407) and SGNASCAR (8843,
9701) they are the same frames at each run and the drawing is not waited
for: frames the machine itself takes 20 to 24 ms to run. Which chip of it
was not measured.

The debug keys (ODIN_DEBUG_KEYS) were not tried with the thread; the
pause, F11, F4, F1 and Escape were, with keys sent to the window, and -s,
-l, -w, -f and -x. F1 used to abort while the machine ran ("IRL pin held
while ICR.IRLM is clear"): the reset before the load cleared the registers
of the SH-4 and raised its pins from chips not reset yet.


HIKARU
------

Move the slave GPU access to src/odin/hikaru_gpu.cpp.

MIE LLE (src/odin/mie.cpp, ODIN_MIE=lle): HACK in src/odin/mie_board.cpp,
port E bits 0-3 read high while the port is an output; the BOOTROM waits
for that at 0x0C00B860 and MAME reads 0xFF there. Measure the pins. The
LLE path is the default; drop the HLE stand-in (mie_stand_in.cpp) once the
JVS inputs (players, coins, analog) are wired to the real board.
The slave reaches the MIE through a window of its own (1A800008 among its
ports, 1B000100 for its bus) and checks it at start: HACK in
hikaru_bus.cpp, a stand-in gives the bus at once and keeps the port as
written. The MEMCTL
of the slave is not run (as Valkyrie had it: no game is known to start a
transfer there).
The backup RAM of the main board (64 KB) starts blank at every run: it is
written to <game>-bram.bin on exit with the other memories and never read
back.
Ports 0x40/0x60/0x80/0x81, touched by the mask ROM init, are logged as
unknown. The serial adapter block (ports 0x09-0x0E) is modelled from
the Z80 code von4 uploads; its reply delays are HEURISTIC (mie.cpp
SERIAL_SEND_CYCLES, SERIAL_REQUEST_CYCLES), measure them on a board.
The CRP-1231 card reader on that line (src/odin/card_reader.cpp,
ODIN_CARD=none|reader) is an HLE stand-in: the LLE path is an H8/300H
core running the dumped firmware (MAME wangmid, crp1231lr10_ver0110.ic2).
Status byte st1 'V' that von4 tests on command 0x33 is not understood.

AICA (src/odin/aica.cpp + arm7.cpp): control block, timers, interrupts,
DMA, RTC and the ARM7 are in; still missing: the 64 slots and the DSP
(sound output), the nested-FIQ question (does reading 0x2D00 drop the
line?), and the HACK in hikaru_bus.cpp (page offset 0 reads 4 for
Pharrier). The ARM7 runs on the 22.5792 MHz bus clock, eight times MAME's
figure: the SGNASCAR cabinet shows its sound driver ready within a frame
of each reload, 20 MHz at least (HEURISTIC: no wait state). It costs a
quarter of the speed: the driver sleeps most of the time, its wait could
be billed without being run.

ROM board 315-5881 (src/odin/crypt_315_5881.cpp, port of MAME, validated
on the real self-test of Planet Harriers and 19.7 M words against MAME's
code): RAM at local bus 0x0A000000, registers at 0x0A010000, keys from
MAME (airtrix, pharrier, swracer). Its ready bit is never busy (MAME).
The BOOTROM board probe answers a constant for the boards without the
chip, braveff and von4 (rom_board.cpp, HACK until those boards are
measured).

SGNASCAR ROM board security FPGA (src/odin/actel_m1.cpp, the stream
decoder of MAME's Naomi M1 board): registers at local bus 0x0A000000, key
from MAME. Proven by the game: it reads 208 bytes at each change of scene,
compares them with its own copy and, at each mismatch, adds 20 to the
weight of the previous picture in the 181 blend. HEURISTIC: every start
reads from the first byte of the mask ROMs (no address register is known),
the ready flag is on bits 31 and 15 of the status, the other bits of the
control and of the status are not known.

GPU textures: the 0D1 UV offsets are added to the vertex UVs the way the
X360 port's vertex shader is assumed to (the Xenos microcode of vo3d was
not decoded: the sign is a HEURISTIC). Texhead filter mode 3 is trilinear
here while the X360 port leaves the sampler defaults (HEURISTIC). The
texel combine field of the 2C1 (bits 31:29) keeps the previous mode in
the port for the values 3, 6 and 7 and means plain texturing here
(HEURISTIC).
The mipmap levels of a texture alternate between the two texture banks,
as Valkyrie had it, where the X360 port keeps them in the texhead bank:
the pages BRAVEFF writes to the texture RAM itself hold the half-size
copy of each bank 0 texture in bank 1, and PODRACE keeps the textures of
the Watto shot of its intro where the level 1 of the same bank would
land (they stay intact on the cabinet).
The texture format 2 holds one 4-bit value a texel, a word being a block
of 2 x 2 texels (the BOOTROM font, 64 x 64 words for 128 x 128 texels);
the four channels get the value (HEURISTIC: the ramp of the PHARRIER
halo, 0 to F, is used for its alpha).
A texture transfer (IDMA) runs to its end whatever is written to its
control register, which reads busy until then (HEURISTIC, from the way
the library polls it; the boot ROM clears the register at a later
interrupt, in the middle of the next transfer of SGNASCAR).
An entry that asks for more bytes than the levels of its size hold gets
those levels and a message: BRAVEFF gives 39 of its 64x32 textures the
bytes of 64x64, twice in its loop, and draws them as 64x32 (HEURISTIC:
what the board does with the rest is not known).
The 2D layers cover the 3D where a pixel has its alpha bit (black
included: the bands of the Racer attract) and are transparent elsewhere
(its green 0x03E0 boxes) and layer 1 sits above layer 2, as the Racer
cabinet footage shows. The bit (bank - 1) of the unit register 3C turns
this keying on; without it the layer covers everything (the white SEGA
screen of von4 on the cabinet). Read from two cabinets only (HEURISTIC).
A layer whose bit of the unit register 38 is clear sits below the 3D,
which shows it where nothing was drawn when bit 0 of the 3C is set and
the clear colour has no alpha bit (the AIRTRIX logo and title screens).
Read from the cabinet footage and the registers of five games
(HEURISTIC).

GPU lighting follows the X360 port's vo3d shaders: the 691 colour is the
material ambient (255 is 1.0 and, unlike the port, a larger value is
brighter: on the PHARRIER cabinet the syringe of the nurse, emission 600
in the shading mode 2, and the selection frame of the shop, ambient 736
in the mode 1, are that much brighter; no other game goes above 255), the
091 colour stands for the texel of untextured polygons, the 491 colour is the specular colour,
the light colour drives both the diffuse and the specular terms,
light vectors arrive in eye space (the library transforms them on the
SH-4), the 881 alpha bit turns
the vertex alpha into a blend factor between 091 and 291 (or the texel),
and the 451 carries the spot
cone (cos outer in 1.15, scale as an 8-bit float; the B61 vector aims it).
sat ((L . axis - cos outer) * scale) addresses the table that the 194 loads
for each light, sixteen points with straight lines between them: SEGA
describes it in its US patent 6,333,742, the X360 port takes the value as
it is. HEURISTIC: the first point sits at the cutoff and the last at the
cone angle, nothing is lit beyond the cutoff, the first of the four tables
of a light is the one used, and 0x400 is 1.0. With the ramp the games load
(0.05, 0.1, 0.2 to 0.5, then 0.55 to 1.0) the wood of the AIRTRIX hall,
4 to 13 % under the cabinet with the plain value, is within 6 % of it.
The scale is the field
read as (1 + mantissa / 16) * 2^exponent (HEURISTIC: the library builds
it as an approximate reciprocal of cos inner - cos outer by subtracting
float bit patterns; with the exact reciprocal the hall-lit scenes of
AIRTRIX are 8 to 12 % darker than the cabinet).
The back of a polygon drawn on both faces (vertex bit 23) is lit with the
reversed normal (HEURISTIC, read from two cabinets: the SEGA logo of
PODRACE is as bright from behind as from the front and dark when its
back turns away from the light, which rules out the absolute value of
N . L; the inner faces of the PHARRIER letters). The back is told by the
winding: turning the normals toward the eye instead darkens the veils of
the PHARRIER galaxy, which the cabinet shows as bright as here. The X360
port emits the second face with the same normal.
Not as the port, read from the PHARRIER cabinet (HEURISTIC): the 491
colour is in 1/64 without the 8-bit wrap of the port (0x40 is 1.0), and
the specular lobe is the reflected ray against the view, raised to the
shininess byte, where the port takes the Blinn half vector (the SEGA
logo seen from the front stays blue, the canopy of the helicopter gets
its highlight).
Not as the port: the attenuation types 2 and 3 fall off on 1 / d and
1 / d^2 with the P and Q of the library (the port does not attenuate
them); the PHARRIER cabinet shows its asteroids untouched by the far
type 3 light of the logo scene.
A parallel light sent with a null direction and no attenuation lights
every side fully (HEURISTIC, read from the BRAVEFF cabinet, whose stages
add such a light, of colour 84 71 71, to every lightset: without it the
control room, the how to play rooms, the demo play and the wall of the
ranking are too dark). Weighing it by the material ambient (691), 0x7F in
the control room, leaves that room too dark whatever the capture curve:
the cabinet frame has more bright pixels than such a render.
The bits 19:16 of the 051 are the ambient level of the light, 0 to 15:
the light editor of PHARRIER shows them as "amb" next to the colour and
SGNASCAR builds them as (int) (16 * level). The light adds level / 16 of
its colour to every polygon it reaches, facing it or not, without the
material ambient, cut by the distance and by the cone like the Lambert
term (HEURISTIC for the scale and the two cuts, read from four cabinets:
with a level of 4 the track of SGNASCAR and its walls turned away from
the sun all gain 0.24 of their texture; the red beacon of BRAVEFF
flashes as strongly as on the cabinet, frame by frame; the board and
the disc of the AIRTRIX how to play; the explosion flash of PHARRIER.
Without the cut by the distance BRAVEFF and PHARRIER get far too
bright). von4 leaves the level at 0 and the X360 port ignores it.
Not as the port either, whose target clamps at 8 bits: a blended colour
is weighted by its alpha before it is clamped, so a lit colour above 1
keeps its excess (HEURISTIC, read from two cabinets: the veils of the
PHARRIER galaxy burn to white and the white floor decals of AIRTRIX, at
an alpha of 0.7, look opaque).

A lightset keeps the numbers of its four lights, and the lights are read
when the set is recalled: the X360 port does so (825C51D0, 825C6090), and
the US patent 6,333,742 of SEGA has the two tables, the groups of four
designations and the lights, read through the Light-ID of the polygon. A
light named three times counts three times (four light circuits in the
patent, four copies in the port). A set never committed has no light, as
in the port; what the board holds there is not known.
The games commit a light again after it was used, up to 76032 times in a
loop (PHARRIER), so a light is not read once the whole list is in. Over the
six loops one mesh in 87 million has a light changed between the recall of
its set and the mesh (RACER, 483FB828): reading at the recall or at each
polygon gives the same lists.
BRAVEFF recalls its set 3 with every light on one frame before it commits
it, when the control room takes fire. From the second loop on that set
still names the lights 6 and 0 of an earlier scene, and the room flashes
for a frame, as on the cabinet.
The light 0 it then shows three times is the light of the SEGA init
program, which a list of BRAVEFF sends again: white, full to 100 and none
at 500, at (100, 100, 100). Its 961 has the bits 14 and 13 set; Valkyrie
read them as "use the old position" and put the light at (0, 0, 0), in the
eye: the room was up to twice too bright. The six loops send that form
with (100, 100, 100) alone, under an identity modelview; what the two bits
say is not known. With the position the door, the walls and the
switchboard are 2 to 4 levels under the footage during the flash, as they
are 1 to 3 under it the frame before.
The 451 of that light is 00000451 01000000: no "no cone" bit in the first
word, where every library light without a cone has 01000451. A cone is
taken when the list sent an axis (B61) as well (HEURISTIC): the init
program sends (0, 0, 1) and its four lights are spots that light nothing
in front; the list of BRAVEFF sends none and its light has no cone. With
the cone on the axis another light left, the room is 3 to 6 levels darker
than the footage.

No attract loop sends a polygon of the types 2 and 3 of the 103, the
shadows: they are drawn as opaque, as in the X360 port, and the command
processor says once when a type never met comes in. The patents (US
6,320,580 and 6,356,264) describe shadow volumes, polygons that are not
drawn and switch the lights off on what they cover: nothing here does it.
The types 0 and 7 are not drawn.

EEPROMs: the main board 93C46 (SH-4 port A, src/odin/eeprom_93c46.cpp)
and the ROM board X76F100 (bus page 0x14, src/odin/x76f100.cpp) are
real and read by the BOOTROM; the MIE 93C46 is in the LLE MIE. It holds
the system settings of the BOOTROM (two copies of 62 bytes and a CRC,
the country in byte 5): ODIN_MIE_EEPROM=<file> loads it and writes it
back on exit. Blank, the BOOTROM falls back on Japan. The other two are
not written back to disk yet. The COUNTRY SETTING page of the BOOTROM
menu is the second routine of its EXIT line: how to reach it is not
found.

Save states: -s <frame>[,...] writes <game>-<frame>.vkstate once that
frame is on screen and -l <file> resumes from it; F4 and F1 do the same
with <game>.vkstate. A state holds the RAMs, every chip, the two 3D
buffers that the blender feeds back and the pictures on their way to the
screen: a resumed run gives the pictures and the memory of a straight one
(checked on the six games, three states each, and on three states saved
while a game loads). It is a raw dump of about 115 MB, tied to the layouts
of the build that wrote it: every item carries its size and another build
refuses the state when one differs. The MIE 93C46 comes back from the
state, not from ODIN_MIE_EEPROM.

Game patches: those of Valkyrie are gone, the six demo loops run without
them: airtrix 0x0C010F9A (660-frame I/O board wait shortened), braveff
0x0C0407D0/0x0C0D522A/0x0C05B53E (forced results, purpose unknown),
pharrier 0x0C01C322 (AICA loop), sgnascar 0x0C0130CE (600-frame I/O board
wait), the BOOTROM EEPROM patch (0x8E6). Kept as a HACK (hikaru_bus.cpp,
Bus::hook): sgnascar 0x0C00BC9A, because the X76F100 image is the Air Trix
one (MAME has no NASCAR dump) and the BOOTROM region check answers
"ERROR 7" with it.

Bridge DMA (memctl.cpp): the transfer completes at the start
write, like MAME. HEURISTIC: the real 315-6154 takes time, but games
refill the source buffer right after starting a transfer (von4 model
loader at 0x0C055FD4), so it must at least run ahead of the SH-4.

Frame (hikaru.cpp): the board keeps its time in SH-4 cycles
(src/odin/scheduler.cpp) and cuts the frame in the lines the game sets:
818 dots a line, 528 lines, 480 of them shown (1A000080, 1A000084,
1A00008C). HEURISTIC: the dot clock is the value of MAME, 25.94729 MHz,
which gives 60.08 frames a second; no game sets or reads it and no manual
gives it. Measure it on a board: PHARRIER keeps the length of its frames,
in microseconds, at 0x0C27D088. HEURISTIC: the frame is the same before
the game sets it, some thirty frames after power-on. Any other screen (the
24 kHz one) stops the emulator. As Valkyrie had them: the chips run a line
each in turn, and the line counter is written at the end of its line.

Interrupts (hikaru.cpp, update_irqs): the lines of the chips are
levels, held until the handler of the game has acknowledged the chip.
IRL1 of the master is the bridge; IRL2 is what the interrupt controller on
ports B, C and F of the MIE lets through (src/odin/mie_board.cpp; the
lines have the names MAME's hikaru.cpp takes from the schematics), and
port A of the master reads the same lines. The master sets the mask of the
MIE to 0xE1: the sound boards do not interrupt the SH-4. The mask of the
command processor (15000084) cuts the causes whose bit is set: the system
library writes 0x72 to 15000088, which clears the causes 0, 2, 3 and 7,
then 0x72 to 15000084. What interrupts is the end of the indirect DMA, the
end of the analysis of a list, and the causes of the GPU 1A (vertical
blanking, end of the plot). Bits 1, 3, 4, 5 and 6 of 15000088 are errors
for PHARRIER, which logs them and recovers (0x0C01B020): none is raised.
Valkyrie read the mask the other way round, raised bit 1 at every frame
and made up an interrupt of the AICA at the end of the frame. The NMI pin
of the slave is bit 0 of the port A of the master, taken at the falling
edge: the slave clears the NMI flag of its DMAC at each interrupt, where
MAME names the pin IRL3.
HEURISTIC: bit 12 of the status of the bridge (04000004) is cleared by
writing 1, the handler writes back what it read; the second sound board
shares the line of the first, the handler acknowledges both; nothing
passes while the MIE does not drive its mask, and pins 4 to 6 of its port
F ask for nothing while it does not drive them; a pin of port A of an
SH-4 that is not an output is high; the NMI pin of the master is held
high; bit 7 of 15000088 follows the causes of 1A000018, which the handler
acknowledges after it; 15000084 is 0 at reset.
Not implemented, the emulator stops with a message: the slave pulling its
line to the master (IRL3 of the master for MAME), a DMA of the bridge of
the slave.

Resets (hikaru.cpp, set_resets): the master drives port D of the MIE
through its window. The system library pulses bit 5 and the sound boards
are reset, their clock aside. The BOOTROM pulses bit 2, the reset of the
slave SH-4, as soon as the MIE gives its bus (frame 27): that one is NOT
done. The slave needs a frame and a half to start again, then clears the
word where the master leaves its first job; the master writes that word
without waiting, after it has copied the game from the ROM board, which
takes no time here: BRAVEFF and PHARRIER write it a frame too early and
never start. It waits for the transfers of the bridge to take their time.
Bits 1 and 4 are the reset lines of the graphics chips (GP_RES, every
chip; PCI1_RES, the display chip): a chip goes back to its power-on state
when its line falls and does nothing until it rises
(src/odin/hikaru_gpu.cpp, set_reset_lines). VON4 pulses both when two
lists in a row did not end (0x0C711C30), then has the slave run a program
it leaves at 14FFF000, sets the chips again and starts over. Here it
happens each time its SYSTEM MENU is left with EXIT (the five other games
reboot through the BOOTROM): the game closes its interrupts 682
instructions after the launch of a last list of 36 instructions, starts
again from its entry point and clears the causes, so the end of that list
is never seen. HEURISTIC: a list ends here at the end of a line; on the
board the end of the analysis may come before the interrupts are closed,
and the game then starts again without the resets. It waits for the real
time of a list. Three things come with the resets:
- HEURISTIC: the command RAM is mirrored up to 14FFFFFF (10FFFFFF for the
  slave), its two upper address lines taken as not wired; only the page at
  FFF000 is seen, and the 8 MB of RAM the RAM test does not reach (IC 33
  to 36) are not placed. The command processor sees it twice as well, at
  48 and at 4C: the system library calls at 4C3FB900 the routine it keeps
  at 483FB900;
- no cause interrupts after a reset until 15000084 is written (0xFF there;
  HEURISTIC for bits 0 to 6): VON4 waits for the frame cause by reading
  1A000018 with its interrupts open, then writes the mask;
- a 0 written to 1A000008-14 acknowledges nothing.
Not known: what the bus reads and writes in a chip whose line is low. The
same routine writes a short list to 120FEE00, the word address the command
processor has for 483FB800 taken for one of the bus: nothing answers there
(48 "unhandled" lines).
The other resets (system, CN4) and a video clock other than the one the
games choose (bits 6 and 7 set) stop the emulator. HEURISTIC: the lines
are high while the MIE does not drive the port.

Main board: bit 0 of 15002000 reads 0, which makes Odin the second main
board, 837-14097, for the BOOTROM (it prints the name in its system menu).
On the first, 837-13402, the bit is set a frame after a list is started,
and the library also takes it, with bit 0 of 1A000024 clear, for a list
that is done. What else differs between the two boards is not known.

Counters: the eleven registers 15002800 to 15002848 (primitives of each
type, texture heads, materials and lights, by the names of the debug menu
of PHARRIER) read 0, nothing says what each counts exactly. AIRTRIX,
BRAVEFF, PODRACE and SGNASCAR copy them to their memory at every list; a
debug text is all that is seen made of them, and the six attract loops
send the same lists when the counters read 1000000. Read as 0 too, for
want of a source: 1A000020 (once at start, 12 times in BRAVEFF) and
1A08006C, which the frame swap of AIRTRIX and VON4 wants at 0 before it
launches a list. Bit 0 of 1A000024, which the games read as busy, drops as
soon as it is written when no list is asked: the libraries write it before
15000058, so it never reads busy during their lists, and SGNASCAR and VON4,
which read it thousands of times a loop, never wait on it (as Valkyrie had
it; what the board answers while it plots is not known). Written and not
understood: 1A000000 and 1A000004 (1 before each launch), 1502C100,
1502C104, 15040E00,
1A0000C4, 1A0000D0, 1A0A1600 at start, and 1A0A1FFC, to which VON4 alone
writes 83709075. Any other register of the two chips is refused with a
message: the six attract loops touch none.


SH-4
----

The two SH-4 run on the Odin core (src/odin/sh4.cpp, sh4_ops.cpp,
sh4_onchip.cpp). It gave the
registers and the memory of the Valkyrie interpreter it replaced at every
slice of the six games. ODIN_SH4_TRACE=<prefix> writes the PC and a hash of
the registers at every slice, ODIN_STATS=1 the host time of each part of the board
and of the drawings.
Tests: tests/sh4.

Speed: 2.3 to 3.6 times the interpreter. What is left is the host missing
its branch predictions, one emulated instruction in three in VON4: a
translator to host code is the next step for the slower machines.

Kept as the interpreter did, each one HEURISTIC, to make right one at a
time once the scenes are compared again:
- the FPU rounds to the nearest; the six games ask for rounding to zero
  (FPSCR.RM = 1) for nearly every instruction, and treat denormals as zero
  (FPSCR.DN), which is ignored too;
- FTRC gives 0x80000000 out of range, the SH-4 saturates; FSCA, FSRRA, FIPR
  and FTRV are computed by the host, the SH-4 has its own tables and error
  bounds; a NaN comes out as the host makes it, the SH-4 gives 0x7FBFFFFF;
- no FPU exception is raised (the slave of PHARRIER enables the division
  by zero);
- the divider of the timers is taken to start with the reset, the
  hardware manual does not say (they count on the peripheral clock, a
  quarter of the CPU clock in clock mode 5, as PHARRIER expects: it takes
  a count for 0.08 microseconds); every mode of a performance counter
  counts cycles, at the end of a slice;
- a store queue write goes to its target at once, PREF sends nothing;
- RFCR and WTCSR read as port A; BCR1 resets to 0x80000000 on both CPUs;
- IRQ10 has the code of IRQ11;
- the DMAC moves its whole count within the write that starts it;
- one instruction is one cycle.

Not implemented, the emulator stops with a message: TRAPA, the exceptions
(illegal instruction, illegal delay slot, address error, FPU disable,
privilege), MAC.L with S set, a write to the cache or TLB arrays, a timer
on the RTC or the external clock, clock ratios that FRQCR cannot give in
whole cycles, an IRL pin held while ICR.IRLM is clear (the four pins as one
encoded level). The input capture of the timers is absent. SLEEP
stops the CPU for good. The MMU and the caches are absent: LDTLB, PREF,
OCBI, OCBP and OCBWB do nothing, MOVCA.L is a plain store. None of this is
run by the six games up to their attract loops. Every code of the software
manual is decoded; FSCA and FSRRA, which it does not list, come from the
SH7091 (MAME sh4.cpp).

A read that nothing answers gives 0 (HEURISTIC: the value of the bus is
not known; BRAVEFF reads FFFFFFFC at 0C076E48). A write that nothing
answers is dropped. PHARRIER makes four of them at every frame, twice:
its copy through the store queues (0x0C011AF0) masks the low bits of the
target away, writes its first 32 bytes below the queues, at DFFFFFE0, and
sends no PREF; it is called for one block.

The clock of the AICA starts on the local time of the host
(src/odin/aica.cpp): two runs do not leave the same byte in the memory of
AIRTRIX. The chip counts seconds, and the software of the Hikaru counts
them from 1964-01-01 (the date routine of the BOOTROM, 0x0C008464, and its
copies in the games), not from 1950 as the Dreamcast does: the CLOCK
SETTING screen showed the right day 14 years late.
ODIN_CLOCK_FILE=<path> is the battery of that clock: the file keeps, in
seconds, how far the clock set in CLOCK SETTING is ahead of the time of the
host, and the board starts from it; without it the board has the time of
the host at every start. Like the settings of the BOOTROM
(ODIN_MIE_EEPROM), it waits for a place of its own on the disk.


GPU / RENDERING
---------------

Graphics chips (src/odin/hikaru_gpu.hpp, wired as on the board). The command
processor, 315-6083 (antarctic.hpp): its instructions are in
antarctic_ops.cpp, the triangles and the display list of a frame in
antarctic_mesh.cpp and antarctic_list.h, its registers and the texture
transfer in antarctic_io.cpp; the renderer only draws that list. The display
chip, 315-6087 (eurasia.hpp): the screen, the layers, the blitter.
GPU_LOG_CP=1 prints each instruction as it is executed (antarctic_disasm.cpp),
GPU_LOG_IDMA=1 each texture transfer, GPU_LOG_DMA=1 each blit.
ODIN_LIST_FP=1 prints a fingerprint of the list of each frame, to compare two
builds; ODIN_LIST_DUMP=<frame> adds one line for each mesh of that frame.
ODIN_PICTURE_FP=1 prints a fingerprint of the picture of each frame.
ODIN_SPEED=1 prints the frames a second of each 60 frames.

The renderer (src/odin/renderer.hpp, OpenGL 4.3) stands for the chips that
plot the polygons. A frame, the two 3D buffers and the composition with the
layers are in renderer.cpp, the passes of a list in renderer_scene.cpp, the
translucent fragments in renderer_translucent.cpp, the programs and the text
of their shaders in renderer_programs.cpp and renderer_shaders.cpp, the
textures in renderer_textures.cpp, the 2D layers in renderer_layers.cpp.
HR_LOG=1 logs the objects of every mesh,
HR_LOG_FRAME=<frame> those of one frame; ODIN_DEBUG_KEYS=1 gives the keys
1 2 3 t u p i j l a d g a switch each (renderer_debug.cpp).

-x N draws the 3D N times finer than the 640 x 480 screen of the hardware
(1 to 6; renderer_buffers.cpp). The clip windows and the 2D layers keep the
pixels of the hardware, each N x N pixels wide. The 501 pattern does not:
its dots are pixels of the drawing, finer than on the cabinet from 2 up,
where they hardly show. The window has a size of its own: -w N asks for N times 640 x
480, cut down to what the display has room for, -f or F11 for the whole
display; the picture of the frame is fitted in it, each pixel of the window
taking the mean of what it covers (a window smaller than the picture
smooths it, which the hardware does not). The screenshots and the picture
fingerprints are those of the picture, not of the window. A save state
keeps pictures of 640 x 480, one pixel of each N x N, whatever the scale it
is made at: the first two frames after a load are coarser. The translucent
fragments take 32 bytes each and their number grows as N x N: VON4 needs
48 MB at 1 and 1.7 GB at 6, under the 2 GB a buffer may have on the
GeForce; a list that does not fit loses fragments and says so. At 6 the
card holds up to 3.3 GB more; a card that runs out of memory stops the
NVIDIA driver, and Odin with it.
Tests: tests/antarctic, tests/eurasia, tests/hikaru_gpu. As Valkyrie had it,
and HEURISTIC: a list is run one instruction a cycle of the SH-4 from its
launch on, the end of its analysis and the end of its plot are told together
when it stops, a texture transfer copies one entry a line and a blit is done
at once. A launch clears the stacks, the bases and the scratch objects
(Valkyrie did at every vertical blanking; HEURISTIC either way, the six
attract loops send the same lists). A jump to itself holds the processor
until the next launch, as BRAVEFF does once at start.

The projection clipping planes are probably scaled by some value, because
using the ones provided by the games as they are leads to a far too close
far clipping plane. The far plane is put at 1e5 (HEURISTIC).

A normal goes through the 3x3 of its modelview, as the position does: US
patent 6,714,690 of SEGA says the geometry processor multiplies both by the
same matrix, the translation switched off for a normal. Valkyrie took the
inverse transposed, which differs when an object is stretched: the droid of
the PODRACE attract was 1.6 times darker than on the cabinet at its chest,
1.1 now; four other zones of PODRACE and PHARRIER moved by a level or two,
none away. AIRTRIX, SGNASCAR and VON4 hardly use such matrices. A modelview
whose 3x3 is null needs no special case any more (a sprite placed by the
last row alone: PHARRIER sends one 21431 times a loop).

The A2BGR10 layers are shown four times as bright (HEURISTIC, as Valkyrie
had it).

Translucent polygons are blended far to near at every pixel, whatever the
triangle or the mesh they come from, without writing the depth (HEURISTIC:
the order of the hardware is not known; read from three cabinets: the
faces of the SEGA letters of the PHARRIER logo, the street lamps of
AIRTRIX, a wall of fire of BRAVEFF). Whole triangles sorted on their
centroid broke those letters. The X360 port reverses its list of objects
instead. The meshes are drawn once, each fragment going to the list of its
pixel; a shader then puts every list in order and blends it, rounding to 8
bits after each blend (HEURISTIC: the precision of the blender of the
hardware is not known; the blender of the host is not used, a GeForce
rounds the wrong way once in 5 to 50 blends). This needs OpenGL 4.3. The
software renderer of Mesa (llvmpipe, LLVM 22.1.3) crashes when it compiles
the shader that fills the lists; AMD and Intel under Mesa are not tried.

The renderer does not wait for the graphics card. The translucent fragments
of a list are counted a frame later, when the card has drawn them; a list
whose buffer of fragments was too small is drawn again, with the textures
it had, before its picture is shown or blended into the next one
(ODIN_LISTS_TEST=1 leaves no room in the buffer: every list is drawn again
and the pictures must stay the same). A texture is deleted a frame after it
was dropped. Not tried on the Intel driver.

A write to the texture RAM drops every decoded texture of its bank. The
levels a texture keeps in the other bank are not followed: a texture whose
smaller levels alone are rewritten would keep the old ones (no case known).

The 101 bias is a signed 11-bit number that moves a mesh of any type but
the background by bias / 65536 of its own depth, nearer when positive
(HEURISTIC, read from three cabinets: four times as much and a light shaft
of AIRTRIX covers the skater; the lettering of the wall and the painted
lines of the SGNASCAR track, on layers of -1024 to -256, show as on the
cabinet; the shadow of the AIRTRIX skater, opaque, comes out of the floor;
the stars of PHARRIER, at -1024, stay behind its asteroids). The unit of
the hardware is not known.

The 161 with bit 18 loads a second modelview and a vertex with bit 12 of
its header follows it (the AIRTRIX trails and skater joints). Bits 13 and
14 of the header take the 3x3 and the last row of that matrix as of the
first: the grid of the BOOTROM C.R.T. test sets the last row alone and has
D in bits 15:12 of its vertices; the six games send F.

The screens of the BOOTROM, read from the lists it sends. A strip goes on
through a material recall and its last vertices take the new material: the
four colour bars of the C.R.T. test are one strip. The 0E8 word names the
texhead of its triangle, from the base: 0 for the text, 1 for the grid, a
texhead that is committed and never recalled. HEURISTIC: the strip is cut
when a list is launched; the texhead number is taken on 16 bits, 0 and 1
are the only ones seen. The SYSTEM MENU and its eleven screens show for
the six games, and so do the test menus of the games with the first screen
of each entry. Not looked at: the pages behind them, CARD R/W CLEANING of
VON4 (a title and nothing else), the sounds.

A row of the 161 changes that row only: PHARRIER sends the four rows with
the first character of a "+50" and the last row alone for the next ones,
BRAVEFF a scale of 0.2 and then the last row of each ember.

The 161 with bit 13 loads a local matrix that the geometry goes through
before the modelview (rows 4 to 6, then F with bits 14 and 19). Read in
PHARRIER: its library entry 0C016076 sends a translation that way, the
game moves the second half of a door from the modelview of the first
(0C0DEA90) and turns the second stroke of a star sparkle by a quarter of a
turn; the cabinet shows the stars. HEURISTIC: the local matrix lasts until
the next row of the modelview and a second one replaces the first; what it
does to the second modelview, and what bits 18 and 19 mean on these rows,
is not known. RACER sets it to the identity in its models.

Triangles are wound after the vertex order and the winding bit, as the
SGNASCAR cabinet shows; the X360 port winds them after the vertex normals,
which is the same for von4 only.

The framebuffer blender (instructions 781, 181). Between the 3D buffers it
works as Sega draws it in US patent 6,549,209 (figures 6, 10 and 11): the
list is drawn apart, blended with the buffer the 781 names and the output
is what the buffer of the list keeps, which gives the motion blur of the
BRAVEFF and PHARRIER cabinets; the rate counts one more from 0x80 up and
each product loses its fraction before the sum; a buffer blended with
itself keeps what it held (BRAVEFF, 781 = 0505 at rate FF: on the cabinet
the screen stays black until the city and its text fade in together).
HEURISTIC: the patent describes an example and nothing else tells that the
chip rounds the same way (a picture under the blur is 1 to 25 levels of
255 darker than with a rounded blend); the alpha of the buffer goes through
the blender like a colour; the emphasis bit of the patent, which keeps a
pixel out of the blend, is not looked for. A 2D layer as a blender input is
done for the PHARRIER logo screens only, on the screen, with the rounding
of the host: check it on more footage.

The polygons of a list show two frames after its launch, once the next
list is launched; the clear color is laid under them on the screen, at
once (the sync routine of the game libraries waits for the end of the
analysis and of the plot; RACER cabinet at 60 frames/s: the SEGA logo two
frames on the black of the next scene, the stars gone one frame before
the layer text). HEURISTIC: the plot of a list fits in a frame whatever
its size; the 781 and the 181 act at once like the clear color; two 3D
buffers are blended when their list is drawn; the textures are those of
the frame of the launch, a frame before the plot.

Figure out the effect of mesh LOD on texturing (normally, and SGNASCAR),
disable mipmaps when appropriate (instruction 0C1 maybe).

The 012 jumps with the conditions 5 and 9 compare the bounding sphere of
the model (561 centre, 055 radius) with a size in 1/16 of a pixel
(HEURISTIC: diameter * projection distance / distance; the unit is read from
the sizes PHARRIER uses, all whole pixels, and the formula from two cabinet
scenes). The 005 tests the same sphere against the view volume of the
current viewport. The 095 compares it with two sizes: SGNASCAR draws the
model plainly on one side, skips it on the other and draws it after a 501
in between; that 501 is the 0 every mesh starts with. Straight after a 055,
with no 095, the condition D jumps when the size is at least I (HEURISTIC:
the SGNASCAR cabinet shows the rows of spectators near the camera; far away
they are too small to tell, and the condition 1 is never used this way).

The 621 sets two kinds of depth queue. With a colour (bit 17 clear) the
materials with the 881 z bit are fogged, as in the X360 port. Without one
(bit 17, two builders of the PHARRIER library, "Depth Queue Start" and
"End" in its viewport menu) the polygons that do not blend fade into what
is behind them, linearly on the eye depth from near / Q to near / Q + 1 / P,
and the background is drawn first for that. Read from the PHARRIER cabinet
(HEURISTIC): its demo play loses the scenery past 1010 units and keeps the
mountains, which the game sends as translucent polygons of alpha 0xFC;
the start scaled by 0.5 or 1.5, or the end by 0.75 or 1.5, moves the
picture away from the footage. Not known: the types 2, 4 and 6 of that queue (a density as P),
whether the punch-through polygons fade like the opaque ones (assumed so),
bit 16, and the types of the coloured queue (ignored like the X360 port).

The 501 sets a pattern of 4 x 4 pixels of the screen and the polygons that
follow are drawn on its pixels only: 32 patterns, read in the X360 port
(823000D0; its shaders kill the other pixels whatever the polygon type),
which the AIRTRIX library addresses as nine levels at four places. Seen on
the cabinets: the combo labels and the message frames of AIRTRIX come and
go in dots, the shadow of the PHARRIER player and the smoke of the BRAVEFF
fire show what lies behind them (both games swap two halves of the pixels
every frame). Not seen on footage: von4, which the port draws this way,
and PODRACE, whose far rocks now come in little by little.
HEURISTIC: the pattern is laid from the top left pixel of the screen, as
the port lays its texture; the low five bits of the word are taken (PODRACE
sends -7 to -1); a subroutine returns with the pattern it was called with,
since BRAVEFF sets one in a model of its burning hall and never clears it
and its cabinet shows what follows whole, but what the chip saves
at a call is not known (it has a second stack pointer, 15000078, that
nothing here uses).

The depth buffer holds log2 (1 + eye depth) / 24, written per pixel: the
projected depth of a near plane at 0.1 or 0.01 was too coarse (black
patches on the cars of the SGNASCAR entry list, the shadow of the AIRTRIX
combo labels drawn over them, the cover of a PODRACE engine over its fan).
The depth format of the hardware and its use of the 421 range are not
known. The top three bits of the 421, which Valkyrie read as a depth
function, number the viewports here: those of a lower number are drawn
first, each over what is there and with a depth buffer of its own
(HEURISTIC: the games use the eight values).

The 221 offsets are signed 16-bit values: von4 centres the view of its
lever panel (tutorial of the U.S.A. setting) on (960, -85), and the levers
land in their panel as on the cabinet. No game sends a negative X: its sign
is assumed (HEURISTIC).

The 154 table gives each material a low and a high alpha threshold through
its C81 index ("Alpha Low Threshold" and "Alpha High Threshold" in the
material editor of PHARRIER; the library writes the entry index XOR 0x20,
with a high of 0xFF, and the entries no game sets hold 0x80 and 0xFFFFFF):
neither is used, the passes test alpha as the X360 port does.

The fourth byte of the 091 colour word is not a field: the material routine
of the libraries of VON4 (0x0C716AC0) and of BRAVEFF (0x0C0D69A0) loads the
three bytes of the colour as a word, and the byte it takes with them is the
red of the 291 kept behind (the same in every material of a loop of the two
games where the byte is neither 0 nor FF). The hose and the masks of the
firemen of BRAVEFF have it between the two and are plain on the cabinet.

That routine makes the words 881, A81 and C81 by adding 0x200 to 0x891 and
takes the 0x10 off the first alone (VON4 has it right, 0x0C716B1A): the
blending mode leaves as A91 with a C91 behind it, 24136 times in the loop
of BRAVEFF and in no other game. A91 is read as its size bits say, two
words, and sets the blending mode from the first: the fires of the game
are then added to the picture as on the cabinet (mean colour of the flames
within 6 % at four instants; a quarter to a half too dark without it).
HEURISTIC: the second word is dropped. The board may run it as a C81
instead; the alpha index it would set is not used.

A 043 without its bit 12 sets the base of the lightsets and recalls the set
too, as Valkyrie had it; a 083 or a 0C3 without theirs sets the base and no
more. The games send it with the index 0 alone (00000043, thousands of
times a loop in SGNASCAR and PHARRIER, and 0E000043 at start), and the six
attract loops draw the same pictures when it does not recall (HEURISTIC).

What the six attract loops send and nothing here reads (the bits of every
command were counted, and set against the comments of antarctic_ops.cpp):
- 881 bit 21, "Highlight Mode" in the material menu of PHARRIER, which uses
  it on two million meshes a loop; the X360 port reads the bits 0 to 4 of
  that byte only. It is not what allows the specular term: nearly every lit
  mesh with a specular colour has it clear.
- 0C1 bits 27:20, a byte of the texture record of the game (0, E0, D0, F0,
  D8, B0, F8... with the filter 1; 10, 20 and 7F too in PHARRIER): the
  bias of the texture level, signed. HEURISTIC: it is taken in eighths of a
  level, on top of the -0.5 of the X360 port, which ignores the byte and
  filters with an anisotropy of 8 (a setting of that console: the patents
  of the chip have trilinear filtering alone, so there is none here unless
  -a <num> asks for it). What is measured: the arena floor of VON4 sends D0
  and its cabinet shows the finest level where a pixel is 25 texels long
  and 2 wide (its picture set against ours with the level forced, blurred
  as the capture blurs the 2D text of the same frame). Sixteenths do not
  reach that with the level OpenGL picks; they would with a level taken
  from the area of the pixel, which a patent of SEGA hints at (US
  6,522,337: the level worked out "on the basis of the size of the
  polygon") and which keeps the grid of the stands of RACER to the far end.
  The videos do not tell the two apart: the 3D of a cabinet is softer than
  its 2D text by about a level whatever the texture.
- 301, once a frame in five games, always 0: the library of VON4 has it at
  the head of a fixed list, with a 101 of 0 behind it (0x0C71234C).
- 2C1 bit 15 (PHARRIER, SGNASCAR), 621 bit 16, 051 bit 23 (the light of the
  aiming reticles of BRAVEFF, lit on its cabinet).
That the X360 port of VON4 skips a field tells nothing of the board: each
of these stays to be found, in the debug menus of the games first (the
material and light editors of PHARRIER gave the names above; the menus of
BRAVEFF and VON4 name their lights and none of these fields; AIRTRIX,
RACER and SGNASCAR have not been searched).
Never sent by the loops, so not judged: the bits 22, 23 and 31:24 of the
881, the depth queue types 2, 4 and 6, the vertices 1Ax, the 0E8, the 081
and the polygon types 0, 2, 3 and 7.

006 and 046 do nothing. Every game sends the pair at the end of the list
that resets the GPU; RACER wraps seven hand-written lists of one flat quad
with them, the 400 stars of its opening text among them, which match the
cabinet as they are. No library emits them, the X360 port skips them, and
no list of the attract loops shows what they change. A whole game of RACER
(course and pod selection, race, walls hit, time out) sends none.

Implement proper model caching using VBOs.


AIRTRIX
-------

Attract mode works.

A wooden board leaning on a brick wall (mesh 408252C4, 42 s into the
cabinet footage) is lit by the spotlight 58 of its lightset in the first
loop after power-on only (frame 8850). From the second loop on the game
commits that light at another place (15369, 22096) and the board is dark,
as on the cabinet. The footage shows a cabinet that has run for a while: a
difference with it is to be looked at in the second loop first.


BRAVEFF
-------

Attract mode works.

